			<div class="content" id="post-content-1750581">
									

<p>After reading VAElynx's post, I understand the article and admire its unique approach and ideas. What I can't admire is the amount of filler. This section&#8230;</p>
<p><em>Calculations suggest the graphene shells can be treated as a composite material in a metastable state with a mean of 105 ± 103 defects at its current temperature and pressure. At 70&#160;K, comparable graphene samples were strong enough to contain sustained pressure of 18&#160;MPa, and impulses of 4.2&#160;GPa-msec.</em></p>
<p>What purpose does it serve? You've already pretty much convinced us of the legitimacy of your fake containment device with all the other paragraphs of jargon that actually contributes to the article. You don't need explanations for your experiment methods, this isn't a scientific journal, it's an SCP article. All this sort of thing does is obscure the point you're actually trying to get across. At least scientific journals have an abstract and introduction, so I can know what the hell is going on.</p>
<p>Edit: After reading it again, I will downvote. I just don't think this article practices economical writing, and the virtues of its approach are fouled up by how obtuse it is.</p>

